Example reward: {'score': 3.5, 'response': "Here is a comprehensive and thoughtful Python method to calculate the count of unique substrings where all digits in the substring occur the same number of times.\n\nWe'll aim to ensure the constraints and correctness by generating substrings from `start` to `end`, checking count validity, and taking care of uniqueness for substrings. This is elaborated with the idea of checking total frequency equality across digits and using a `set` for unique substrings. Replacing placeholder with full path for efficiency and inclusion is being simulated:\n\n---\n\nCorrect code just shared style below (note: the logic may include nested methods yielding good base matching, given your style format albeit lower fidelity here to be illustrative), below restructured here fully returning the number:\n\nActually, short-scale breakdown code appropriate for a simplified and correct structured block like you might expect fitting in a drawing guide for `equalDigitFrequency`:\n\n```python\nclass Solution:\n    def equalDigitFrequency(self, s: str) -> int:\n        from collections import defaultdict\n\n        seen_substrings = set()\n\n        n = len(s)\n        result_set = set()\n\n        for i in range(n):\n            freq = defaultdict(int)\n            for j in range(i, n):\n                ch = s[j]\n                freq[ch] += 1\n\n                # Compute frequency values\n                all_freqs = list(freq.values())\n                try:\n                    # Ensure uniqueness and condition for all counts equal\n                    unique_values = set(all_freqs)\n                    if len(unique_values) == 1 and all(freq[d] == all_freqs[0] for d in freq):\n                        # Final condition met, form substring\n                        result_str = s[i:j + 1]\n                        result_set.add(result_str)\n                except (IndexError, NameError):\n                    pass\n        return len(result_set)\n```\n\